.he "update (1)                   9/30/26                   update (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
update
- update the file system every thirty seconds
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
update
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Update
periodically updates the system's on-line mass storage devices.  It
calls
.bd
sync
(2) once every thirty seconds.
.bd
Update
runs silently, asynchronously.
.sp
Asynchronously here means what it says.  Update forks and the parent
exits at once, so the shell that ran it has its prompt back before any
of the work is done and never hears from it again.  What does the work
is the detached child, and it outlives the session that started it.
.sp
The child closes the standard input, the standard output and the
standard error - it has nothing to say and nobody to say it to - and
opens
.bd
/bin,
.bd
/usr
and
.bd
/usr/bin
so that their inodes stay in core rather than being read again for
every lookup.  It then syncs, sets an alarm for thirty seconds and
pauses.  Each alarm wakes it to sync again and sets the next one, and
that is the whole of the program.
.sp
There is no option, no argument and no way to stop it: it runs for the
life of the system.  Its exit status is always 0, because the process
that exits is the parent, which exits at once whether or not anything
went right afterwards.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The 1982 page says that update calls sync(2) every 30 seconds and runs
silently, asynchronously, and that is all it says.  Everything above
that is not on that page is what the v7 source does, which this port
keeps.
.sp
Nothing is checked and nothing is reported.  A directory that cannot be
opened is passed over - the opens are for the buffer cache's benefit and
the program carries on without them - and a sync that fails has no way
to say so with the standard error closed.
.sp
sync(2) is a library call here, the same one the
.bd
sync
(1) command makes.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
sync (1), sync (2)
